# Carrier Wound #27: A Deterministic Planner-Output Miss Wore A Transient Label

Wound #26 (the unbounded normalize grind that rode a blind SIGKILL) shipped and
deployed. We re-drove a dream-shape run on the fixed code —
`run-live-20260614T154437382Z-3ad88e36` — and the planner LANE ran clean:
`sandbox.exec` exited 0, pi finished, normalize-output returned. **Wound #26 is
confirmed dead.** The lane did its job and pinned an output.

Then the run BLOCKED anyway with:

```
blocker.code    = adapter_unavailable
blocker.message = "Dynamic workflow planner failed"
```

…carrying a ZodError that reported all four top-level blueprint keys —
`harness`, `machine`, `plan`, `verificationContract` — as **undefined**. The lane
ran, returned, and what it returned was **schema-invalid**: pi emitted some JSON
object that was not a blueprint.

That is a *content* failure, and it is **deterministic**. Re-driving cannot fix
it — the planner runs only on the first drive (idempotent via `run/plan.json`),
and a planner failure pins no plan.json, so every DO-alarm re-drive re-runs the
planner, gets the same wrong-shape output, and re-fails identically. The
"re-drove for 40 minutes" loop. The carrier had two lies stacked on top of a
recoverable truth:

1. **Misclassification.** It labeled a deterministic content failure as
   `adapter_unavailable` — the code reserved for transient transport faults, the
   one that invites blind retry. This UNDID Wound #25's adapter honesty: the
   adapter named the failure correctly, then `workflow-app.ts` caught *every*
   planner error and flattened it back to `adapter_unavailable`.
2. **Discarded diagnostic.** The blocker threw away *which* keys pi actually
   emitted. The operator saw "planner failed," not "pi emitted `{result}` where
   the contract wanted `{harness, machine, plan, verificationContract}`." Blind.

> If the system can determine in advance that it will fail at an operation, it's
> always better to fail fast. That way, the caller doesn't have to tie up any of
> its capacity waiting; it can get on with other work. […] Can there be any
> bigger waste of system resources than burning cycles and clock time only to
> throw away the result?
>
> — Michael Nygard, *Release It!*, §5.5 Fail Fast

A schema-invalid blueprint IS a fail-we-can-determine-in-advance. We were burning
40 minutes of clock re-throwing the same dice.

## Mechanism — honest at the adapter, flattened at the application

The ZodError originated at the adapter's FIRST parse site
(`cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts`):

```ts
PlannerLaneBlueprintDocumentSchema.parse(readJson(outputPin))
```

The normalizer "succeeded" (it found *some* JSON object) but that object wasn't a
blueprint. The bare `.parse()` threw a ZodError. Upstream,
`workflow-app.ts` had a single catch that mapped EVERY thrown planner error to
`blocker("adapter_unavailable", "Dynamic workflow planner failed: …")`. One
flatten erased the distinction between "the sandbox couldn't be reached" and "the
sandbox returned garbage."

## Fix — a typed contract error that names present-vs-missing keys

New denial code in `schemas.ts`: `planner_output_invalid` (between
`plan_node_config_invalid` and `drive_stall_reaped`).

**Application layer** (`ports.ts`, zod-free, precedent
`StaleDriveGenerationError`): a typed `PlannerBlueprintContractError` carrying
`stage` (`"planner-output" | "blueprint-assembly"`), `presentKeys`,
`missingKeys`, `issuePaths`, `runId`, `workItemId`. Its message names the present
top-level keys, the missing required keys, and the schema issue paths, and marks
the failure **deterministic**. `redactionSafeTopLevelKeyNames` returns only
`Object.keys(value)` — NAMES, never values; safe to surface to the operator.

**Infrastructure layer** (`cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts`): both parse sites
converted from `.parse()` to `.safeParse()` + a typed throw via the
`plannerBlueprintContractError` factory (which derives present/missing keys from
the REAL schema error, not a hand-craft). Site 1 = `planner-output` (pi's raw
pinned output); site 2 = `blueprint-assembly` (after the supervisor attaches
`plannerLane`).

**Application catch** (`workflow-app.ts`): discriminated —
`PlannerBlueprintContractError` → `block("planner_output_invalid", error.message)`;
`z.ZodError` (defense-in-depth, in case any unconverted site leaks) →
`planner_output_invalid` with computed `issuePaths`; everything else → keeps
`adapter_unavailable` (genuine transport). The dependency arrow stays correct:
infra throws the application-layer typed error; application keys on it.

The reclassification is load-bearing because `block()` writes
`terminalBlocker.code = blockedBy.code` → the operator status endpoint's
`blocker.code` flips directly from `adapter_unavailable` to
`planner_output_invalid`. The status page now tells the truth.

## Chaos tests that would have caught it (4 new, faithful doubles)

`tests/integration/workflow-app.test.ts`:

- **"blocks a deterministic planner-output contract miss as
  planner_output_invalid (not adapter_unavailable)"** — planner double rejects
  with a real `PlannerBlueprintContractError`; asserts blocker + terminal blocker
  both `planner_output_invalid`, message marked deterministic and naming the keys.
- **"keeps a generic planner transport failure classified as
  adapter_unavailable"** — planner double rejects with a bare
  `Error("sandbox dispatch failed: connection reset")`; asserts the transport
  path still maps to `adapter_unavailable`. Guards against over-reclassifying.

`tests/integration/cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.test.ts`:

- **end-to-end, faithful hostile double** — pins a wrong-shape `{result:{…}}`
  object as the REAL lane output through the REAL `createPlannerRuntimeHarness`,
  drives the REAL `createCloudflarePiPlannerLaneAdapter.proposePlan`, asserts it
  throws a `PlannerBlueprintContractError` with `stage:"planner-output"`,
  `presentKeys:["result"]`, `missingKeys:[harness, machine, plan,
  verificationContract]`. No polite mock — the production schema does the
  rejecting. (Polite-fakes franchise wound: doubles politer than prod reproduce
  nothing.)
- **unit, factory** — feeds the REAL schema's parse error from a real wrong-shape
  object to `plannerBlueprintContractError`; asserts it derives present/missing
  keys from the actual schema error.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · `pnpm vitest run` = **418 passed**.

## What this does NOT claim (skepticism held)

The root cause of *why pi emitted a non-blueprint object* is still UNCONFIRMED.
The `{result:{…}}` shape was observed once; it could be a wrong-extraction header,
a refusal envelope, a truncation, or a prompt-contract drift. This fix is correct
under ALL of those hypotheses: it stops the blind retry loop and surfaces the
REAL `presentKeys` pi emitted. The NEXT live run's honest diagnostic will name the
true root cause instead of us guessing. We did NOT reclassify all ~19
`adapter_unavailable` sites — only the class with a live receipt.

## Follow-ups (carried, not yet done)

- Deterministic re-drive fast-fail: a `planner_output_invalid` block should not
  re-run the planner on the next DO-alarm — fold into the pause/re-arm seam so a
  deterministic content block stops re-driving entirely (true Fail Fast).
- Pre-existing zombie run (separate).
- Input-size fast-cap for normalize (Wound #26 follow-up).

## The carrier discipline (sixth+seventh wound, same shape)

Read consistency (#26 follow-up) and now error *classification* are both carrier
properties: the lane can run perfectly and the carrier still lies about the
outcome. "Observe the run, don't stare at a status page" only works if the status
page is HONEST. A transient label on a deterministic failure is the carrier
telling the operator to keep waiting for something that will never change.
